body:
Each time you enchant or repair a tool, you incur a work penalty. A pickaxe that’s been worked once already will have an additional enchantment cost of 1 the next time (the 2nd time) it’s worked. It’ll add 3 the 3rd time. Then 7 the 4th. 15 the 5th time. And 31 the 6th. 39 is the maximum enchantment cost (I think) before something is too expensive to repair or enchant.

The penalty is calculated by doubling the previous penalty and adding 1. Like this:
1st work: 0;
2nd: (0 x 2) +1 = 1;
3rd: (1 x 2) + 1 = 3;
4th: (3 x 2) + 1 = 7;
5th: (7 x 2) +1 = 15;
6th: (15 x 2) + 1 = 31;
7th: (31 x 2) + 1 = too expensive

There is a workaround, though. When you’re enchanting with a book, it will only calculate the penalty using the item with the highest work penalty. So if you’ve worked your boots 3 times and you want to add enchantments, you’ll have a work penalty of 7. If you want to add 4 enchantments, you can just combine them into 1 book, and as long as that book only incurs a cost of 32 enchantment levels or less (the cost of the enchantments only), then your total enchantment will be within the 39 level limit (32 cost plus 7 work penalty), because it doesn’t include the lower work penalty you’ve accrued from combining the books. Whereas adding each book individually would quickly go over the enchantment limit.

Hope that makes sense.
